Title: Emit flat run artifacts directly in exp2 naive arm
Created: 2026-05-23
Author: copilot

--- log ---
2026-05-23T07:35Z copilot created — output cleanup showed producer currently emits one-file run subdirectories and requires post-run flattening

--- body ---

## Context

The Exp2 naive arm producer (`experiments/sota/exp2_naive_arm.py`) currently
writes each production run's raw payload into a per-run subdirectory
(e.g., `.../mistral_run01/mistral_probe.raw.json`) while metadata and narrative
are written at top level (`mistral_run01.json`, `mistral_run01.md`). This yields
an awkward 2-files-plus-1-file-directory shape per run and requires manual
post-run restructuring.

There is currently no Makefile target orchestrating this arm with a cleanup
step, so layout consistency depends on manual file moves.

## Actions

1. Update `experiments/sota/exp2_naive_arm.py` so production runs write a flat
   3-file set directly at top level:
   - `<agent>_runNN.json`
   - `<agent>_runNN.md`
   - `<agent>_runNN.raw.json`

2. Preserve probe behavior as explicit probe snapshots, but keep naming
   unambiguous (current vs legacy/provenance).

3. If this arm gets Makefile orchestration, add a dedicated target with
documented output paths and no post-hoc restructuring requirement.

4. Update docs/comments in the script and output README language so emitted
   artifact contracts match actual behavior.

## First test

Add a unit/integration test that runs the naive-arm writer in a temp output
directory with mocked providers and asserts that, for `n>1`, no `*_runNN/`
subdirectories are created and `*_runNN.raw.json` files exist at top level.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Producer emits flat 3-file production artifacts directly
- [ ] No one-file run subdirectories are created for production runs
- [ ] Probe outputs remain preserved and clearly named
- [ ] Tests enforce flat output contract for future changes
- [ ] Any orchestration docs/targets reflect the final artifact layout

## Out of scope

- Re-running Exp2 naive-arm experiments solely to rewrite historical outputs
- Changing scientific metrics or classifier behavior
